cloud APIs, and three different ways to be told no

1 · a public data endpoint, no authentication
    GET ip-ranges.amazonaws.com  →  200 OK · 2,754,637 bytes · 1757.5 ms

2 · a control-plane endpoint, unsigned
    GET ec2.us-east-1.amazonaws.com  →  400 Bad Request · MissingParameter · 1116.7 ms
    The request must contain the parameter AWSAccessKeyId

3 · the same call, signed with AWS's published example key
    signature sent     : fc30f11631b44b1541162b7b020b0f9f…
    scope              : 20261117/us-east-1/ec2/aws4_request
    GET ec2.us-east-1.amazonaws.com  →  401 Unauthorized · AuthFailure · 669.6 ms
    AWS was not able to validate the provided access credentials

what the three answers mean
    public   : no identity needed — this is data AWS publishes on purpose
    unsigned : rejected at parameter validation, before authentication starts;
               the API names what is missing (AWSAccessKeyId)
    signed   : the Authorization header was parsed and the credentials evaluated,
               then refused — the key is AWS's published example, not a real one

wrote api_probe.json
